{"text":"A policy analyst writes a sentence describing an empirical framework that relies on transparent data. At first, the sentence simply calls the framework empirical. The analyst notices that this wording describes a method but does not show whether the researchers themselves claim that orientation. If the commitment is part of how they present their work, the analyst revises the sentence to make that self-identification clear. The revision does not change the data; it changes what readers can understand about the framework’s declared stance.

The analyst then reads the sentence alongside the rest of the argument. In academic writing, a declared methodological commitment can frame how evidence is presented and interpreted. Here, identifying the framework’s orientation helps readers distinguish a chosen approach from a neutral description supplied by someone else. It also gives them a basis for considering whether that approach shapes the analysis. The analyst keeps the reference close to “empirical,” where it clarifies the attribute being claimed, rather than treating it as a general assurance that the entire argument is free of bias.

Finally, the analyst considers the audience. Naming a commitment can make the writing more transparent, but repeated emphasis on a stated position may seem performative or may lead readers toward an interpretation before they reach the evidence. The analyst therefore uses the signal only where the distinction matters: at the point where the framework’s self-presentation helps readers assess its methods and claims. A reader encountering the finished passage can see both the empirical orientation and the fact that it is a commitment the researchers identify as their own, while remaining free to question how that orientation guides the interpretation.","source_facts_used":["An empirical framework can rely on transparent data.","The term marks a claim or attribute that is deliberately presented as the speaker’s own.","In academic writing, it distinguishes declared self-presentation from neutral description.","It commonly modifies an adjective describing a stance or method.","Declared commitments can frame evidence and interpretation.","Such wording can support transparency while inviting critique of possible bias.","Overuse can seem performative or predispose interpretation.","Policy analysis is a context in which the term is used."]}

{"text":"A policy analyst revises a paragraph that describes her framework as pragmatic. She realizes that readers could take the adjective as a detached description of the method, rather than as a position she has chosen to foreground. To make that distinction, she places the relevant stance-marking adverb immediately before “pragmatic.” The resulting two-word modifier presents the framework through a known methodological alignment and guides readers to consider the evidence and interpretation in light of that alignment.

She then checks whether the wording fits the document’s audience. In this setting, the modifier gives the paragraph rhetorical force: it does more than describe the framework, because it signals the analyst’s relationship to the label. That can support a candid account of methodological commitments in policy analysis, while also inviting readers to examine how those commitments shape the argument. The analyst keeps the wording near the first explanation of the framework, where it can frame the discussion without repeatedly interrupting later analysis.

During revision, she notices that several later sentences use similar labels for the report’s approach. She removes most of them. Repeating such markers could make the writing seem performative or could lead readers to treat the document as merely confirming its own bias. She retains the single modifier because it has a clear job: it distinguishes self-presentation from neutral description. If the paper instead discussed ideology or identity, the same construction could work before terms such as “liberal,” “secular,” or “feminist,” provided the writer intends the label to shape how readers understand the stance. The analyst’s final paragraph therefore uses the adverb once, next to the adjective it qualifies, and lets the rest of the evidence carry the argument.","source_facts_used":["The term is an adverb that marks the manner of expression and an intentional stance.","It commonly appears before adjectives, including labels such as pragmatic, liberal, secular, and feminist.","It distinguishes deliberate self-presentation from neutral description.","It can signal methodological, ideological, or identity-based alignment with a known label or doctrine.","It frames evidence and interpretation around declared commitments and can guide reader interpretation.","It is used in policy analysis and supports discussion of framing, stance-taking, and bias transparency.","It has evaluative and rhetorical force and may invite critique of the stated stance.","Frequent use can seem performative, polemical, or bias-affirming."]}

{"text":"The butcher has dressed a rabbit and now prepares its carcass for further processing. First, he identifies the animal’s outer covering as the material that needs to be separated from the carcass. He works around the carcass, freeing that exterior layer rather than treating it as part of the meat. As he proceeds, he continues the separation until the covering is no longer attached. The task is complete when the outer material has been taken away from the carcass.

Next, he sets the removed material aside for processing. This connects the immediate work to a broader use of animal coverings: hides have been processed into leather, and ancient tanning and hide processing established durable leather technologies. The butcher’s action is therefore part of preparing an animal material, not an attempt to change the rabbit’s appearance or apply a decorative surface. The material can be understood in terms of its later use as a hide or in leather-making, while the carcass has been prepared through the separation.

The same word for an animal’s covering can also refer to a biological boundary with protective and sensory roles, but that is not the task underway here. In this practical situation, the butcher handles the covering as material to be removed during preparation. The sequence is straightforward: begin with the dressed carcass, separate its exterior covering, finish detaching it, and direct the resulting material toward further processing. This illustrates how a covering that has a biological role can also become a material used in cultural and craft contexts.","source_facts_used":["A butcher skins a rabbit after dressing.","Animal coverings include tangible materials such as hides and leather.","Ancient tanning and hide processing established durable leather technologies.","Animal coverings serve biological functions including protection and sensation.","Animal hides and leather have material and cultural uses."]}

